<h2 class="doc-h2">Can it become Material, Cupertino or Fluent?</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    Partly, and the boundary is sharp. A design language is not a palette: the parts that live in
    <b>tokens</b> transfer completely, and the parts that live in <b>component markup</b> do not
    transfer at all. The three themes in the switcher are real attempts, with the gaps named.
</p>

<div class="doc-example">
    <div class="doc-preview block">
        <Table>
            <TableHeader>
                <TableRow>
                    <TableHead>What</TableHead>
                    <TableHead>Material 3</TableHead>
                    <TableHead>Cupertino</TableHead>
                    <TableHead>Fluent 2</TableHead>
                </TableRow>
            </TableHeader>
            <TableBody>
                @foreach (var (what, md3, cup, flu) in Rows)
                {
                    <TableRow>
                        <TableCell Class="font-medium">@what</TableCell>
                        <TableCell>@md3</TableCell>
                        <TableCell>@cup</TableCell>
                        <TableCell>@flu</TableCell>
                    </TableRow>
                }
            </TableBody>
        </Table>
    </div>
</div>

<p class="doc-note">
    <b>Cupertino is the one that essentially works</b> — Apple's system is colour, radius,
    spacing and type, with no state-layer model and almost no shadow, so very little of it needs
    to live in markup. <b>Material gets close enough to read as Material and is not Material</b>:
    its colour and shape map cleanly, its state layer is approximated with a pseudo-element, and
    the ripple has no honest substitute. <b>Fluent</b> is mostly colour and its very small radii,
    which change the character more than you would expect; the reveal highlight and acrylic are
    out of reach.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Writing one</h2>
<div class="doc-example">
    <pre class="doc-source"><code>/* themes/mine.css — then set data-theme="mine" on &lt;html&gt; */
[data-theme="mine"] {
    --background: oklch(98% 0 0);
    --foreground: oklch(15% 0 0);
    --primary: oklch(55% 0.2 265);
    --primary-foreground: oklch(100% 0 0);
    --accent: oklch(95% 0.01 265);        /* the hover ground — easy to forget */
    --border: oklch(90% 0 0);
    --radius: 0.75rem;
    --radius-control: 0.5rem;
    --control-h: 2.5rem;
}
[data-theme="mine"].dark { /* … */ }</code></pre>
</div>

<p class="doc-note">
    Six things decide most of how a theme reads, in roughly this order:
    <code>--radius</code> and <code>--radius-control</code>; <code>--control-h</code> and
    <code>--control-text</code>; whether <code>--card</code> differs from
    <code>--background</code>; <code>--elevation-*</code>; <code>--primary</code> and
    <code>--accent</code>; and <code>--border</code>. Then check every component, not only the
    ones you changed — <code>--accent</code> is the hover ground for ghost buttons, menu rows,
    table rows and the select panel at once.
</p>

@code {
    static readonly (string, string, string, string)[] Rows =
    [
        ("Colour roles", "yes", "yes", "yes"),
        ("Corner shape", "yes, needs --radius-control", "yes", "yes"),
        ("Elevation", "yes", "yes (barely used, which is the look)", "yes"),
        ("Motion", "yes", "yes", "yes"),
        ("Density", "yes, 40dp", "yes, 44pt", "yes, 32px"),
        ("State model", "approximated with ::before", "nothing to reproduce", "focus bar added as a rule"),
        ("Signature interaction", "no ripple", "n/a", "no reveal, no acrylic"),
        ("Type scale", "no, 15 named roles", "tracking only", "no, 9 ramps"),
        ("Corner curve", "—", "no squircle in CSS anywhere", "—"),
    ];
}
